/**
@page vector_dispatch Vector dispatch and the tiers

@tableofcontents

LAME has hand-written SIMD versions of its most time-critical quantization
loops. At run time, LAME selects them from the instruction sets that the CPU
reports. This page describes the tiers, how a dispatch site selects a routine,
and four decisions:

- There is no SSE4.1 or SSE4.2 tier.
- @c calc_sfb_noise_x34() has an SSE2 version only.
- The AVX-512 tier has the quantization loop, but not the table search.
- The ARM tier has one routine. The x86 tiers have seven.

Each decision is based on measurements. The page @ref vector_dispatch_record
records them.

@section vector_dispatch_ladder The tiers

@c vector_implementation() returns a @c vector_impl_t. It names the tier of
this encoder instance. Each dispatch site tests whether this tier is at least
the tier that its routine needs. So a routine that exists only in a lower tier
is also used on a machine with a higher tier. There is one selector and one
enumeration. To add a tier, add one branch in the selector and one row in the
table in @c util.c. That table is the only place that lists the vector routines
of a build.

The tiers depend on the architecture:

<ul>
<li><b>x86: scalar, SSE2, AVX2, AVX-512.</b> There is no tier between SSE2 and
AVX2. Every x86-64 CPU has SSE2, so on x86-64 the SSE2 tier needs no run-time
check. Most 32-bit x86 CPUs made since 2001 also have SSE2. On 32-bit x86, a
run-time check decides, unless the build already targets SSE2. AVX2 and
AVX-512 are always checked at run time, unless the build already targets
them.</li>
<li><b>ARM: scalar, NEON.</b> The NEON tier has one routine. Every AArch64 CPU
has Advanced SIMD, so on AArch64 no check is needed. On 32-bit ARM it is
optional, and a run-time check decides.</li>
</ul>

The option @c --vector names the set to use: @c --vector @c none selects the
scalar code, and @c --vector with the name of a set selects that set, even if
the CPU offers a wider one. The old option @c --noasm still works. It selects
the same thing, and it prints a warning that it is deprecated.

@subsection vector_dispatch_reach How a dispatch site gets the tier

A mistake here is invisible, so this section states it in full. The tier gets
to a dispatch site in one of two ways. Only the first way follows
@c vector_implementation() automatically:

<ul>
<li><b>Directly from the instance.</b> The call site of @c quantize_xrpow()
passes @c vector_implementation(gfc). When the selector returns a new tier,
@c quantize_xrpow() gets it at once.</li>
<li><b>Through a function pointer.</b> The table search is not called directly.
@c huffman_init() stores one of @c choose_table_c, @c choose_table_sse2,
@c choose_table_avx2 or @c choose_table_neon in @c gfc->choose_table. Each of
these wrappers passes a fixed @c vector_impl_t to @c choose_table_x(), and
@c choose_table_x() passes it to @c ix_max_v() and to the counting routines.
A tier without its own wrapper never gets to these routines, whatever the CPU
reports and whatever the user selects.</li>
</ul>

Because of the second way, a tier can look complete and still never run its
table search in an encode. The CPU check, the table row, the enumeration value,
the compiled routines, the exported symbols and passing unit tests are all not
enough. A bitstream comparison between two tiers also cannot show that the
higher tier ran, because a routine that never runs gives identical output. A new
tier needs a check that <em>fails</em> if its routines are missing, not a check
that passes when nothing changes.

@section vector_dispatch_no_sse4 No SSE4.1 or SSE4.2 tier

For every routine on this page, an SSE4.1 or SSE4.2 version is not faster than
the SSE2 version, and is usually slower.

The reason is the data. The table search (@c ix_max(), @c choose_table()) finds
the largest quantized value of a region and adds up code lengths from tables.
Every value it reads is at most @c IXMAX_VAL @c = @c 8206, which is below
2<sup>15</sup> = 32768. So all values fit into signed 16-bit lanes:

- <b>SSE2</b> has the 16-bit integer instructions that this needs:
  @c pmaxsw (maximum), @c pminsw (clamp), @c packssdw (packs two 32-bit vectors
  into one 16-bit vector) and @c pmaddwd (computes the table index in one
  instruction). Each instruction works on <b>eight lanes</b>.
- <b>SSE4.1</b> adds @c pmaxsd, @c pminsd, @c pmulld and @c pextrd. These work
  on 32-bit lanes, so each instruction works on only <b>four lanes</b>. For
  values that fit into 16 bits, this is half the width for no gain.

SSE4.2 adds only string and CRC instructions. None of them helps the encoder,
so for this work SSE4.2 is the same as SSE4.1.

The float routines (@c quantize_lines_xrpow(), @c calc_sfb_noise_x34()) also
gain nothing from SSE4.x. SSE2 already has all single-precision float
instructions they use. The float instructions of SSE4.1 (@c dpps, @c blendps,
@c roundps, @c insertps) do things these routines do not do.

So a CPU with SSE4.2 and no AVX gets the full table-search gain from the SSE2
tier. The FreeBSD test host of the project is such a CPU (a Westmere Xeon).

@subsection vector_dispatch_saturation The 16-bit limit

@c packssdw saturates at 32767. So the 16-bit maximum can replace @c ix_max()
only for the way @c choose_table() uses the result. @c choose_table() only
compares the result with @c IXMAX_VAL and with the @c linbits limits.
@c IXMAX_VAL @c = @c 8206 is far below 32767. So every value that matters is
exact, and every larger value becomes 32767, which is also above
@c IXMAX_VAL. A compile-time assertion in @c takehiro.c stops the build if
@c IXMAX_VAL is ever raised to 32767 or more. This works for this one caller,
not in general.

The ARM routine uses 32-bit lanes and does not narrow the values, so this limit
applies to the x86 tiers only.

@section vector_dispatch_no_vbr_avx2 calc_sfb_noise_x34() has SSE2 only

@c calc_sfb_noise_x34() estimates the quantization noise of a scalefactor band.
Only the VBR code in @c vbrquantize.c calls it. This is the default VBR mode.
AVX2 and AVX-512 versions were written and measured.
Neither was faster, so neither is in the tree:

- Each block of four values needs two table lookups that depend on the data.
  A wider vector does not make these lookups faster.
- The sum of squares is computed in a fixed order, so that every CPU of an
  architecture gives the same bits. A wider vector must keep this order, so it
  speeds up only the cheap arithmetic around the lookups.
- VBR encodes spend most of their time in the psychoacoustic model, not in
  this routine.

The details and the numbers are in @ref vector_dispatch_record_vbr_avx2 and
@ref vector_dispatch_record_experiment.

@section vector_dispatch_avx512 The AVX-512 tier

The AVX-512 tier has one routine: the quantization loop
@c quantize_lines_xrpow(). Only @c count_bits() calls it, and only
@c quantize.c calls @c count_bits(). So CBR, ABR and the old VBR mode
(@c --vbr-old) use the AVX-512 tier. The default VBR mode does not use it.

Measured on three CPUs, the tier gives about 6.5% at 320&nbsp;kbit/s on Ice
Lake, nothing on Zen 5, and costs about 1% on Zen 4. It is enabled on all of
them. The dispatch tests only what the CPU can run. It never tests the vendor
or the model, so the selected routine always follows from the instruction sets
alone.

The tier has no table search and no @c calc_sfb_noise_x34(). Both were written
and measured, and then removed:

- The AVX-512 @c calc_sfb_noise_x34() did not give the same result as the SSE2
  version, and it was 6.6% slower at @c -V2 on Ice Lake.
- The AVX-512 table search was faster on Intel at @c -b @c 320 and slower on
  Zen 4. No setting enables it correctly for every CPU. VBR also uses the table
  search, so enabling it for one mode only would need a mode test in the
  dispatch.

The numbers are in @ref vector_dispatch_record_avx512 and
@ref vector_dispatch_record_experiment.

@section vector_dispatch_arm The ARM tier

The x86 tiers have seven hand-written routines. The ARM tier has one:
@c count_bit_esc_neon(), the counting loop for the escape tables. It gives
about 3.5% on a 320&nbsp;kbit/s encode and about 1% on VBR.

Each of the seven x86 routines was checked for ARM:

- For three of them, clang already produces equivalent vector code from the
  plain C on AArch64, so no NEON version was written.
- For two of them (the FFT and the counting loop without escape), a NEON
  version was written and measured. Both were slower than the code clang
  produces from the plain C, so both were removed.

The details are in @ref vector_dispatch_record_arm.

@section vector_dispatch_gaps Why each routine is missing

The tiers miss routines for four different reasons:

- <b>SSE4.1 and SSE4.2:</b> no version was written, because the instructions
  give nothing over SSE2 for this data.
- <b>AVX-512 @c calc_sfb_noise_x34():</b> written twice and measured. It lost
  both times.
- <b>AVX-512 table search:</b> written and measured. The result depends on the
  vendor, so no setting is correct for every CPU.
- <b>Most ARM routines:</b> the compiler already produces vector code that is
  as fast or faster.

Only the first reason is a lack of measurement. The other three are measured
results. The code is gone, and @ref vector_dispatch_record keeps the results.
*/

/**
@page vector_dispatch_record Vector dispatch: the measurement record

@tableofcontents

This page records the measurements behind the decisions on
@ref vector_dispatch. It is a record: it says what was measured, on which
machines, and what the result was. The current state is on
@ref vector_dispatch.

@section vector_dispatch_record_sse4 SSE4.1 against SSE2

Measured on a Core i7-10700 with GCC 16.1, in ns per value at a region length of
576:

- The region maximum is about a quarter faster as 8-lane SSE2 than with the
  SSE4.1 instruction @c pmaxsd (0.058 against 0.082 ns).
- The table lookup is about 3.0 times as fast as scalar with SSE2, and only
  about 1.8 times as fast with SSE4.1.

SSE4.1 is slower in both parts.

@section vector_dispatch_record_vbr_avx2 AVX2 for calc_sfb_noise_x34()

An AVX2 version was written and measured on a Core i7-10700. For a whole VBR
encode it ran at 1.00&times; &plusmn; 0.01 against the SSE2 tier, with both GCC
and MSVC. This is inside the measurement noise. The same AVX2 tier gives a real
6&ndash;8% on the CBR routines. Three causes add up:

1. <b>Table lookups.</b> Each block of four values needs two table lookups
   that depend on the data: @c adj43[] in the quantization step and @c pow43[]
   in the noise step. AVX2 can do each as one eight-wide @c vgatherdps. On
   Skylake and older cores, @c vgatherdps is microcoded and loads about one
   value per cycle, so it is no faster than scalar loads. It only removes the
   shuffles that put the values back together, and those were never the cost.

2. <b>The order of the sum is fixed.</b> So that every CPU of an architecture
   gives the same bits, the sum of squares is computed as
   (x0&sup2;+x2&sup2;)+(x1&sup2;+x3&sup2;) for each four values, and added to a
   running scalar sum. An AVX2 version that works on eight values must still
   add its two halves separately, in this order. An eight-wide accumulator
   would change the order of the additions, and the output would then depend on
   the CPU. So the wider vector helps only the cheap multiply, subtract and
   square around the two expensive lookups.

3. <b>Small share.</b> The SSE2 version of this routine changes a whole VBR
   encode by only about 6% with MSVC and by about 0% with GCC. GCC already
   vectorizes the scalar loop well. VBR spends most of its time in the
   psychoacoustic model, not here. The gain from AVX2 is a part of that small
   share.

A routine may exist in one tier only. Each extra tier costs a source file, a
build entry, a dispatch branch and a test. A tier that shows no gain is not
worth that cost.

@section vector_dispatch_record_avx512 The AVX-512 tier

No machine of the project has AVX-512, and QEMU's TCG does not emulate it. So
the code was compiled with four toolchains, the disassembly was read
instruction by instruction, and unit tests were written. Then a test kit went to
the lame-dev list. Three members ran it on their own machines and timed whole
encodes.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_built What was built

The tier was built in two stages, because the stages had different risks:

- <b>Stage 1:</b> the run-time check, and AVX-512 versions of the table search
  (@c ix_max(), @c choose_table()) and of the quantization loop
  (@c quantize_lines_xrpow()). These routines work value by value, or compute a
  maximum, which does not depend on the order. So a wider vector cannot change
  the output.
- <b>Stage 2:</b> @c calc_sfb_noise_x34(), in two versions. One kept the fixed
  order of the narrower tier exactly. The other added up freely, and was built
  only to measure what the fixed order costs.

Only the quantization loop is in the tree now. The table search and stage 2 were
removed after the measurements below.

While the measurements were open, the tier was built only in alpha versions.
Since the measurements, the tier is built wherever the compiler can build it,
and selected wherever the CPU can run it, like SSE2 and AVX2. It needs no
configure option and no version test.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_tested What was tested, and where

Three machines ran the kit on Linux. They encoded 16-bit stereo 44.1&nbsp;kHz
music. This was the first time these instructions ran anywhere:

| CPU | microarchitecture | compiler | what it ran |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| AMD Ryzen 9 7950X3D | Zen 4 | GCC 13.3 | both stages, tests and timings |
| Intel Core i5-1035G1 | Ice Lake, client | GCC 14.2 | both stages, tests and timings, then a second run under a benchmark harness |
| AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D | Zen 5 | GCC 15 | stage 1, timings only, under a harness |

- <b>Unit tests, stage 1: passed</b> on both machines that ran them. The
  routines give the same results as the scalar code for every region length,
  every limit and clamp value, the largest and smallest index, and odd tails.
- <b>Encoder output, stage 1: bit-identical</b> on both machines that ran it,
  at @c -V2 and at @c -b @c 320. A full encode with the tier gave the same
  bytes as the same build limited to the tier below. This also tests the parts
  that the unit tests do not test, for example the count of clamped values in
  @c count_bit_ESC(). Stage 1 is correct.
- <b>Unit tests, stage 2: the same single failure</b> on both machines, with
  two vendors and two compilers. @c calc_sfb_noise_x34() did not give exactly
  the result of the narrower tier. Its sum selects a scalefactor, so its test
  requires the exact result, not a result within a tolerance. The difference
  came from the sum: the fixed order of the four-value blocks did not survive
  the change to sixteen lanes. A tier that changes the existing output is not a
  candidate, whatever its speed.

The <em>encoder</em> output of stage 2 was bit-identical anyway, on both
machines. This is a warning, not a reassurance. The unit test runs the routine
with inputs that an encode does not happen to produce, so the unit test finds
more. Identical output on one clip does not prove that two tiers agree. The
exact test is the one to trust. A second test checks that the test harness
<em>can</em> report a difference. It passed on both machines, so the failure is
in the routine, not in the test.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_speed The measurement

Each run is a whole encode after a warm-up. It compares the AVX-512 tier with
the AVX2 tier, selected with @c --vector @c avx512 and @c --vector @c avx2.

| CPU | stage | @c -V2 | @c -b @c 320 | runs per arm |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |
| Zen 4 7950X3D | 1 | within the spread | <b>1.0% slower</b> | 3 |
| Zen 5 9950X3D | 1 | <b>neutral</b> | <b>no difference</b> | 10 / 25 |
| Ice Lake i5-1035G1 | 1 | within the spread | <b>7.6% faster</b> | 3 |
| Ice Lake i5-1035G1 | 2 | <b>6.6% slower</b> | <b>6.5% faster</b> | 25 &times; 2 orders |

The last row decided both questions, and it has the best method of all rows.
It ran under a benchmark harness that reports the spread, with 25 runs per arm,
on 16-bit stereo 44.1&nbsp;kHz music, on mains power. Then it ran again with
the two arms in the opposite order. The same arm in both positions differs by
0.00% to 0.25%. So the order of the arms does not matter, and the noise floor of
that machine under a harness is a quarter of a percent. In all four comparisons,
the means are 24 to 40 standard errors apart.

The two results go in opposite directions:

- <b>CBR: the tier wins, about 6.5% at 320&nbsp;kbit/s.</b> The row is a stage 2
  build, but this number measures stage 1. The only routine of stage 2 is
  @c calc_sfb_noise_x34(), and only @c vbrquantize.c calls it, so it cannot run
  in a CBR encode. Both builds run the same instructions here. The earlier
  figure from three runs on the same machine, 7.6%, has the same sign and about
  the same size.
- <b>VBR: stage 2 loses, about 6.6% at @c -V2.</b> This was the first VBR
  measurement under a harness on any machine. Every "within the spread" above
  comes from three runs one after the other. The loss comes from the routine of
  stage 2, not from stage 1: Zen 5 ran stage 1 alone under a harness, and
  measured 1.004&nbsp;s against 1.006&nbsp;s at @c -V2. The routine of stage 2
  is the inner loop of VBR, while the table search of stage 1 runs three times
  per granule.

The two AMD results stay the same. On Zen 4, every AVX-512 run was slower than
every AVX2 run. This was repeated with a second input file, so it does not
depend on the clip. On Zen 5 the harness measured 1.655&nbsp;s against
1.655&nbsp;s, at &sigma;&nbsp;0.005&nbsp;s. The two arms were equal, not just
close.

Stage 1 at @c -V2 on Intel was later measured directly on the Ice Lake machine,
with 25 runs per arm in both orders. The AVX-512 tier against AVX2 at @c -V @c 2
gave <b>-0.17% and +0.17%</b>, against a noise floor of 0.32%: neutral.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_why Why the wider vector gains so little

The obvious explanation for the AMD results is the datapath. Zen 4 runs a
512-bit operation as two 256-bit halves, so a sixteen-wide instruction does no
more work per cycle than the eight-wide one it replaces. This is true for Zen 4,
but it is not the reason. Zen 5 is reported to have a wider datapath in its
desktop parts, and Zen 5 measured zero, not a small gain. If the width were the
limit, a wider path would have shown a gain.

What remains are properties of the loops, not of a CPU:

1. <b>The same two limits as for AVX2 above.</b> The table search is limited by
   @c IXMAX_VAL, and the SSE2 tier already runs it on eight 16-bit lanes. The
   band noise routine is limited by its table lookups, and its sum must keep the
   order of the narrower tier. A wider register does not change any of this.
   Stage 2 shows it: a wider vector only helps if the order of the sum changes,
   and changing the order made the result wrong.

2. <b>Small share.</b> After AVX2, only a small share of the encode time is
   left to gain. A part of a small share is smaller than the spread between
   runs.

The halved datapath of Zen 4 is a minor detail. It explains why Zen 4 is slightly
slower and not exactly equal.

None of this explains the gain on Ice Lake. Its cause is not known. One
possible cause: a mobile core that issues few instructions per cycle gains from
half the instruction count, even when the datapath is not the limit, while the
much wider front end of Zen 5 gains nothing from fewer micro-operations. This
fits every number above, but so did the datapath explanation, until the Zen 5
result disproved it. So it is only a hypothesis. Hardware counter data per
routine on an Ice Lake CPU could decide it, by showing whether the gain follows
the micro-operation count or the memory behavior. Nobody has measured this yet.
The question does not block anything. It matters more for the next
microarchitecture than for this tier.

Intel client CPUs have AVX-512 only for a few generations: from Ice Lake to
Tiger Lake and Rocket Lake. Alder Lake and later client CPUs do not have it,
until AVX10. So the CPU with the gain is not typical of current client
hardware. Zen 4 and Zen 5 are most of the CPUs that use this tier, and there it
gains nothing or costs about one percent. The tier costs nothing to keep, so it
stays, but it is not expected to matter often.

@section vector_dispatch_record_experiment The removed AVX-512 table search and band noise

Both routines existed. They were written, unit-tested, and kept behind an
environment variable until testers could measure them on CPUs with AVX-512.
Then they were removed. This section records why, and why a third attempt needs
new reasons.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_experiment_what What was measured

Each routine had its own environment variable, in alpha builds only. So a run
could enable one routine and leave the other off. Then each routine is measured
by a difference <em>within one run of one binary</em>, without any effect from
a second run:

<ul>
<li><b>arm 3 &minus; arm 2</b> = the table search alone</li>
<li><b>arm 4 &minus; arm 3</b> = the band noise routine alone</li>
</ul>

Testers who own the hardware ran this on two microarchitectures, with 25 runs
per arm, in both orders, on builds of the same revision. A positive time means
that the routine <em>costs</em> time.

<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Ice Lake (i5-1035G1)</th><th>Zen 4 (7950X3D)</th></tr>
<tr><td>table search, @c -V @c 2</td><td>+0.99 / +0.92 %</td><td>+0.86 / +0.76 %</td></tr>
<tr><td>band noise, @c -V @c 2</td><td><b>+5.63 / +5.57 %</b></td><td>-0.85 / -0.40 %, at the noise floor of that machine</td></tr>
<tr><td>table search, @c -b @c 320</td><td>-1.5 %, a gain</td><td>+2.5 %</td></tr>
</table>

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_experiment_why Why they were removed

<b>The band noise routine costs time.</b> On Ice Lake the cost is seventeen
times the measured noise floor of the machine. On Zen 4 it cannot be told apart
from zero. It is not a gain on any machine at any setting. The reason is the
one given for AVX2: the sum keeps a fixed order of four-value blocks, so that
every CPU of an architecture gives the same bits. So a wider register does not
speed up the sum. The extra lanes only add the cost of filling them. This comes
from the contract of the routine, not from a microarchitecture. A third attempt
needs a different contract, not newer hardware.

<b>The table search cannot be enabled on its own.</b> It is not used by VBR
only: @c noquant_count_bits() calls it, and VBR calls that function too. So
enabling it "for VBR" would need a mode test in the dispatch, and the dispatch
has no mode tests. Its own results also depend on the vendor: a gain at
@c -b @c 320 on Intel, and a cost on Zen 4. So no setting enables it correctly
for every CPU. The routine is not slow. There is just no rule that enables it
correctly.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_experiment_lesson What the experiment taught

Three lessons remain after the code is gone.

<b>An experiment must be reachable from an encode, even before it can be
measured.</b> For weeks the routines were in the tree, but no wrapper installed
them. So no encode on any machine ran them, and the field results of that time
measured only the quantization loop. A routine that nothing calls is not "not
yet measured": its measurements are about something else.

<b>The switch must report itself.</b> Each arm printed in the @c --verbose
header which experiment it ran. So a report from a run that did not take the
path could be told apart from a run that did. Without this, a missing path
gives a wrong result instead of a visible gap.

<b>The test setup is code too.</b> The first @c -V @c 2 results from both
testers measured an uncached @c getenv() in the inner loop of the switch:
5.9 million calls per encode. They looked like a 10% AVX-512 loss on two
vendors. The bug was in our test code, and it was found on a machine without
AVX-512. When a measurement of an experiment is surprising, check the setup of
the experiment before the CPU.

@section vector_dispatch_record_arm The ARM routines

Each of the seven x86 routines was checked: does a hand-written NEON version
beat the code that the compiler already produces on AArch64?

- Three routines needed no NEON version. clang produces equivalent vector code
  from the plain C. This includes a 32-bit signed maximum. AArch64 has this
  instruction. SSE2 does not, and that is the only reason the SSE2 region
  maximum narrows to 16 bits.
- Two routines were written, measured and removed: the FFT and the counting loop
  without escape. Both were slower than the code clang produces from the plain
  C.
- @c count_bit_esc_neon(), the counting loop for the escape tables, is in the
  tree. It gives about 3.5% on a 320&nbsp;kbit/s encode and about 1% on VBR.

One missing instruction shapes this routine. The routine builds a table index
from a pair of values, <em>x * 16 + y</em>. The x86 version narrows the region
to 16 bits, so that one @c pmaddwd turns four pairs into four indices. ARM has
no instruction that multiplies and adds adjacent pairs, at any width. So the
NEON version multiplies by <em>(xlen, 1)</em> per lane and then adds pairs. This
needs no narrowing, so the 16-bit limit does not apply. For this reason, the
16-bit assertion in @c takehiro.c is for the x86 tiers only.

The routine adds the register halves with @c vpadd_s32, not with
@c vpaddq_s32, @c vuzp1q or @c vuzp2q. Those are AArch64 only, while
@c vpadd_s32 is in the ACLE for both A32 and A64. 32-bit ARM is the reason that
the tier is checked at run time, so the routine must build there.
*/
